<section class="px-5 py-20 md:px-8 md:py-24 lg:px-12 lg:py-32">
  <div class="container-main">
    <div class="max-w-3xl">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.08em] text-violet-ink uppercase">
        Engineering
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] md:text-5xl">
        Correctness is part of the product.
      </h2>

      <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        The core workflows are designed around a small set of invariants:
        inventory changes need evidence, sales must commit as a complete unit,
        and values such as price and authorization cannot be trusted simply
        because the client supplied them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16 grid gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-border bg-border lg:grid-cols-2">
      <article class="bg-background p-6 md:p-8">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.08em] text-violet-ink uppercase">
          01 — Inventory ledger
        </p>

        <h3 class="mt-4 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
          Stock is derived from movements.
        </h3>

        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Products do not carry an independently editable stock field. Receipts,
          sales, and adjustments create signed StockMovement records, and
          current inventory is derived from those movements.
        </p>

        <div
          class="mt-6 rounded-md border border-border bg-soft-silver p-4 font-mono text-sm leading-7"
          aria-label="Inventory calculation"
        >
          <div>receipt → positive movement</div>
          <div>sale → negative movement</div>
          <div>adjustment → signed movement</div>
          <div class="mt-3 border-t border-divider-light pt-3">
            current stock = Σ quantity delta
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="bg-background p-6 md:p-8">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.08em] text-violet-ink uppercase">
          02 — Transaction boundary
        </p>

        <h3 class="mt-4 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
          A sale succeeds as one operation.
        </h3>

        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          TindaTrack creates the sale, line items, corresponding inventory
          movements, and audit evidence inside the same database transaction. If
          required validation fails, the operation does not leave partial sale
          state behind.
        </p>

        <ol class="mt-6 space-y-3 text-sm">
          <li class="border-l border-violet-ink pl-4">
            Load authoritative product data
          </li>
          <li class="border-l border-violet-ink pl-4">
            Validate availability and calculate totals
          </li>
          <li class="border-l border-violet-ink pl-4">
            Create sale and sale items
          </li>
          <li class="border-l border-violet-ink pl-4">
            Create stock movements
          </li>
          <li class="border-l border-violet-ink pl-4">Write audit evidence</li>
        </ol>
      </article>

      <article class="bg-background p-6 md:p-8">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.08em] text-violet-ink uppercase">
          03 — Server authority
        </p>

        <h3 class="mt-4 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
          The client does not decide the price.
        </h3>

        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Sale requests contain product identifiers, quantities, and a payment
          method. The backend reloads the current products, reads their selling
          prices, calculates each line total, and derives the final sale total.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="rounded-md border border-border p-4">
            <p class="text-sm font-medium">Client sends</p>
            <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-sm text-muted-foreground">
              productId
              <br />
              quantity
              <br />
              paymentMethod
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-md border border-border p-4">
            <p class="text-sm font-medium">Server decides</p>
            <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-sm text-muted-foreground">
              unitPrice
              <br />
              lineTotal
              <br />
              totalAmount
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="bg-background p-6 md:p-8">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.08em] text-violet-ink uppercase">
          04 — Authorization
        </p>

        <h3 class="mt-4 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
          The API remains the security boundary.
        </h3>

        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Authentication uses bearer JWTs, but a valid token is not treated as
          permanent proof that the account should still have access. Protected
          requests re-read the user and reject accounts that no longer exist or
          have become inactive.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          OWNER and STAFF permissions are enforced by backend authorization,
          while frontend route restrictions primarily improve the interface
          experience.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="dark bg-night-canvas px-5 py-20 text-light-text md:px-8 md:py-24 lg:px-12 lg:py-32">
  <div class="container-main">
    <div class="grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-12">
      <div class="lg:col-span-4">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.08em] text-lavender uppercase">
          Integrity under contention
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em]">
          Two requests cannot sell the same final unit.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="lg:col-span-7 lg:col-start-6">
        <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-dark">
          Sale processing uses Serializable transaction isolation and retries
          retryable transaction conflicts. The repository also contains a
          concurrency verification that starts two sales against a product with
          exactly one unit remaining.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-8 grid gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-divider-dark bg-divider-dark sm:grid-cols-3">
          <div class="bg-dark-surface p-5">
            <p class="font-mono text-xs text-lavender uppercase">
              Starting stock
            </p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold">1</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-dark-surface p-5">
            <p class="font-mono text-xs text-lavender uppercase">
              Concurrent sales
            </p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold">2</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-dark-surface p-5">
            <p class="font-mono text-xs text-lavender uppercase">
              Successful sales
            </p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold">1</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-muted-dark">
          The expected invariant is one successful sale, one rejected sale, one
          sale item, one matching SALE movement, and final stock of zero. The
          losing request ultimately resolves to insufficient-stock validation
          rather than allowing inventory to become negative.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
